trace_agent_message line=12 phase=commentary text=I’ll first inspect the HumanEval prompt, translator limits, and supplied K modules. Then I’ll implement the smallest semantics-compatible solution, build concrete and symbolic definitions, and run the exact proof commands that will go into `prove.sh`.
trace_agent_message line=26 phase=commentary text=The task is `is_simple_power(x, n)`. The most proof-friendly total implementation is repeated exact division, with explicit guards for `x == 1`, `x < 1`, and `n <= 1`; this avoids the `n == 1` nontermination trap and cleanly covers all integer inputs, not only the examples. I’m now matching the proof state to the supplied frame/loop semantics.
trace_agent_message line=69 phase=commentary text=The proof will have two universal claims: an inductive loop claim for all `x ≥ 1, n > 1`, and an end-to-end entry-point claim for all integer arguments. The specification’s `simplePower` predicate is the standard repeated-division characterization of “there exists a nonnegative exponent,” with explicit total cases for out-of-domain and degenerate bases.
trace_agent_message line=173 phase=commentary text=The inductive loop claim is now closing; the remaining failure is at the end-to-end claim’s `x == 1` branch because the Haskell simplifier does not specialize a symbolic argument from the path condition before reducing the recursive predicate. I’m adding the equivalent unconditional base equation `simplePower(1, n) = true`, which preserves the definition and gives the prover the needed syntactic reduction.
trace_agent_message line=224 phase=commentary text=Both claims are being checked together now. The prover has passed parsing and symbolic execution and is spending time in arithmetic/path implication; I’m letting this run briefly, then I’ll split the claims if needed to isolate any remaining branch.
trace_agent_message line=337 phase=commentary text=The arithmetic characterization is no longer the issue; the combined run is exploring both circularities at once and is not converging quickly. I’m isolating the claim labels and will prove the loop lemma and entry-point theorem in separate positive commands so each run has a bounded proof search.
trace_agent_message line=506 phase=commentary text=The proof decomposition is now working as intended: the loop induction prints `#Top`, the three finite guard partitions print `#Top`, and the positive-domain entry-point theorem prints `#Top` while using the separately proved loop claim as its lemma. I’m adding an LLVM assertion harness for all prompt examples, then I’ll run the complete recorded script from a clean set of generated definitions.
trace_agent_message line=614 phase=final_answer text=Implemented and verified: - [solution.py](/work/solution.py) and generated [solution.mpy](/work/solution.mpy) - [verification.k](/work/verification.k) - [spec.k](/work/spec.k) with five exhaustive claims - [prove.sh](/work/prove.sh) `./prove.sh` exited 0. LLVM execution passed all prompt examples, and all three positive `kprove` commands printed `#Top`. The script also checks that the proved K module exactly matches the translated solution. RESULT: KPROVE_PASSED — Every required positive proof command printed #Top and exited 0.
